deep.reconstruction:
Context: in 1986 Grothendieck is building an axiomatic 'geometry of (topological) shapes'. The 1-dimensional building block is a set L with a family S of 'segments' I. Each segment has a closure Ĩ and a two-point boundary ∂I. The interior is Ĩ° = Ĩ \ ∂I. For two points a ≠ b inside a segment there is a sub-segment I_{a,b} with ∂I_{a,b} = {a,b}.

The previous page calls L a 'variété bisignée' if L = Ĩ for some (necessarily unique) I ∈ S. One endpoint x0 ∈ ∂I is chosen and x1 is the other.

This page (numbered 9) fixes notation for x ∈ L. The 'closed interval' [x0,x] is:
- {x0} if x = x0;
- Ĩ = L if x = x1;
- Ĩ_{x0,x} (the closure of the unique sub-segment from x0 to x) if x is interior.
[x,x1] is defined symmetrically by swapping x0 and x1.

Proposition: for x, y ∈ L the following are equivalent:
- (i) [x0,x] ⊂ [x0,y];
- (ii) x ∈ [x0,y];
- (i bis) [x,x1] ⊃ [y,x1];
- (ii bis) y ∈ [x,x1].

Write x ≤ y for this relation. Reflexivity and transitivity are 'evident' from (i) or (i bis), so it is a preorder. It is even an order. If [x0,x] = [x0,y], then either x = y = x0, or the two segments I_{x0,x} and I_{x0,y} have the same closure. They are then equal, and taking boundaries gives {x0,x} = {x0,y}, so x = y.

The next page completes the picture. The order has least element x0 and greatest element x1. It is dense ('infiniment divisible') and locally filtrant. The segment structure is recovered: sub-segments are the pairs x < y, with closure {z : x ≤ z ≤ y} and boundary {x,y}. Conversely, a dense bounded order defines such a structure.

Purpose: show that an abstract 1-dimensional model, a closed 'segment', is the same thing as a dense total order with endpoints. Order-theoretic and segment-theoretic descriptions of the 1-dimensional model are thus interchangeable. This underpins a tame, combinatorial ('modérée') description of shapes.
